00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.260
on our journey to Cloud
Services Platform.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.151
A little history
lesson here is about we

00:01:36.151 --> 00:01:37.150
started with mainframes.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.770
We started with big boxes.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.830
And every evolution
since then has

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.170
been trying to move further
and further up the stack.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.587
So along came virtual machines
which [INAUDIBLE] the hardware

00:01:47.587 --> 00:01:49.420
and allowed you to spin
up multiple machines

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.200
on the same host.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.084
And then containers
came into popularity.

00:01:54.084 --> 00:01:55.750
Now containers have
actually been around

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:56.740
for quite some time.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.980
Google has been working with
containers for over 15 years.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.490
But we didn't really start
to see widespread adoption

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.480
until Docker.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.390
Around 2013, when Docker
unveiled their stack

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.449
for a lightweight container
runtime with great packaging

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.190
portability and
deployment semantics

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.190
around these environments
where you could really

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.170
package up your application
interdependencies

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.840
and move it from
host to host, that's

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.510
where we started to see
containers actually becoming

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.620
a reality.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.579
But of course, Docker
solved the problem

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.120
of taking an app
and its dependencies

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.179
and deploying it
onto a single host.

00:02:30.179 --> 00:02:31.970
That was never going
to be the end journey.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.010
We always needed something
a little bit more.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.100
And this was around the
time that the team started

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.170
to think about Kubernetes.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.890
So how do you take not
just a single container

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:43.890
on a single host--

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.680
how do you take
multiple containers

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.500
across multiple
hosts and orchestrate

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.260
that over a fleet
of virtual machines?

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.990
So Google started to take
all of the lessons we've

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.240
learned from our internal
container management system,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.260
Borg, externalize
those with Kubernetes.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.190
But as [INAUDIBLE] said
in the day one keynote,

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.800
Kubernetes was also
never the end state.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.310
It really is that consistency
layer between infrastructure

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.380
and then higher level services.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.370
It allows the operators
to think about what's

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.370
provisioned underneath
and allows the developers

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.320
to just worry about
having consistent tooling

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.849
between the two.

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:21.890
And a lot of the announcements
that you guys have

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.450
seen at GCP Cloud Next have been
focused on these higher level

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.710
services.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.480
So we talked about
Knative in the keynote

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.520
this morning, which offers
serverless and some paths level

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.630
functionality that can
run on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.690
We announced Istio
1.0 GA, which is

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.730
the service mesh capabilities
to actually think

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.980
not just about
Kubernetes deployments,

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.387
but how services connect
and talk to each other

00:03:45.387 --> 00:03:47.720
along with how you can control
connections between those

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.850
with security and
everything else.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.670
And then there's not tools
that just Google has,

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.100
but the community has built
in the open source, as well.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.740
So we have CIDC tools.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.170
We have monitoring and logging
agents like Prometheus.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.419
We have things like
the Open Service Broker

00:04:02.419 --> 00:04:04.460
where we actually can
connect to services running

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.080
in Cloud using an open API.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.415
And our customers came to
us and they said, well,

00:04:11.415 --> 00:04:13.040
we love these open
source [INAUDIBLE],,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.001
but we want you guys
to manage it for us.

00:04:15.001 --> 00:04:17.209
And this is where we started
offering hosted versions

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:18.450
of all of these solutions.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.410
So Kubernetes became
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:22.850
our hosted managed offering.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.701
We run that on top of
our infrastructure stack.

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:27.200
And a lot of the
announcements today

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.912
came out for serverless
for GKE and Istio for GKE.

00:04:29.912 --> 00:04:31.370
These are managed
offerings that we

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.286
want to install inside
your Kubernetes cluster

00:04:33.286 --> 00:04:35.750
so that you can think about
the important thing, which

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.820
is application
development, and not

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.710
having to manage infrastructure.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.920
But of course, just like
one host on a container

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.440
wasn't enough and we
needed Kubernetes,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.080
we're now entering the realm
of Multi-Cluster Management

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.040
management.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.930
So one cluster is not enough.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.410
We have to be able
to spread across

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.210
different clusters potentially
in different environments.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.570
And this is where
Matt and team have

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.580
been doing a ton
of thinking around

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.400
what are the tools
and processes in place

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.290
that people can use to
manage multiple clusters?

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.160
So single pane of
glass UI, policy

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.395
syncing between clusters, CI/CD,
service discovery, and even

00:05:15.395 --> 00:05:16.770
some demos that
we're showing off

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:20.040
today of doing multi-cluster
ingress using global load

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.166
balancer and federating
out to multiple clusters.

00:05:24.166 --> 00:05:25.790
But the big announcement
that we talked

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:29.690
about is really saying, OK,
if we can do this on GKE,

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.970
can we do it across
environments?

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.940
And this is where the
ideas for GKE On-Prem

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.770
started to come into play.

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:40.190
Customers said, we want those
management capabilities of GKE

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.400
and all of the service
layers on top of that.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.490
And we want you to run
it in our data center.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.100
And so this is what we've been
working on for quite some time

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.530
and trying to really flesh
out the product and the vision

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.150
of how we can take
all of the bits

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.630
and experience
we've done with GKE

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.590
and move that into an
on on-prem experience.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.705
And most of the announcements
on day one and day two

00:06:02.705 --> 00:06:04.580
were focused on this
whole family of products

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.610
that we've called Cloud
Services Platform that includes

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.780
more than just Kubernetes.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.240
It includes all of the tools
that sit on top of Kubernetes,

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.777
as well.

00:06:13.777 --> 00:06:16.110
And with that, Matt's going
to go into a little bit more

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:19.759
of a deep dive on what it means
to do Multi-Cluster Management.

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:20.800
MATT DE LEO: Thanks, Wes.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:21.300
Yeah.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.490
So let's take a quick
step back and discuss

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.670
what we're seeing as far as why
people need multiple clusters

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.160
in the real world.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.900
It is many, many reasons.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.106
So the first one
we see a lot is,

00:06:34.106 --> 00:06:35.730
I need to provide
low latency services.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.840
So I've got customers
all over the globe.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.390
And I need to be able to
serve them with low latency

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.970
very quickly from wherever the
closest cluster is to them.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.590
You can do this today with the
Google Cloud Load Balancer.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.487
We have a tool that we
build called Kubemci.

00:06:51.487 --> 00:06:53.070
And we're working
on some other tools,

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.610
as well, to allow
you to do this.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.920
In addition to that, people
often say, you know what?

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.170
I really want to have highly
available service running

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:02.320
across regions.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.070
So if I have an outage
in say, US Central,

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.632
I want my traffic to failover
to the next closest region.

00:07:08.632 --> 00:07:10.590
This is something, again,
that you can leverage

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.520
Google's network to do.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.640
And it's something that Kubemci
and multi-cluster ingress,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.430
in general, support.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:21.720
And in addition, we
often see that customers

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.470
have this data locality issue.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.970
So if I've got a customer
that's traveling from--

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.970
an end user traveling from one
location to another location--

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:32.040
I need to serve them traffic--

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.860
there's traffic to the cluster
that actually has their data.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.590
That's another
important use case.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:44.160
The other one we see a lot-- and
this is more on the developer

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:44.990
side--

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.617
is I need to separate my
teams or maybe my tenants.

00:07:47.617 --> 00:07:49.950
Or maybe we want to have
different environments for dev,

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.045
staging, and prod.

00:07:51.045 --> 00:07:52.920
And when you want to do
that, you necessarily

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:53.878
need multiple clusters.

00:07:56.442 --> 00:07:57.900
The other one we
see is if you want

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.725
to implement a multi
cloud-- or we'll

00:07:59.725 --> 00:08:02.100
get into hybrid in a second--
but a multi cloud strategy.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.710
You also want to use
multiple clusters.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.840
Nobody, as far as I know, really
has a Kubernetes distribution

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:13.110
that can span multiple
providers and still be reliable.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.330
And finally, we often
see that the customers

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.690
want to try a new version
of Kubernetes safely.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.340
And they'll try to do it
via Canary in clusters.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.340
And so they'll actually
deploy the same workload

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.805
on a new cluster-- a new
version of Kubernetes--

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:27.930
and then they want to
transition the workloads over

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:30.790
from one cluster to another.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.480
So that's why people would
want to use multiple clusters.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.580
But what about hybrid?

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.159
So the cloud, we've
determined, must collaborate

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.200
with your on-prem And
that's exactly why we're

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.510
building GKE On-Prem.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:45.060
We see a lot where you've
got legacy dependencies

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:45.810
on premises.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.910
You might have an
old database that

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.351
really can't move to the cloud.

00:08:49.351 --> 00:08:50.850
You might have some
services running

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.530
there that really
need to stay on-prem

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.850
for a variety of
regulatory reasons.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:56.220
And those need to stay there.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.220
And you need to be able to
access them from the Cloud.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.530
Another reason is
local execution.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.530
So you might want to
have a cluster running

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:09.780
or your services running in a
warehouse on a factory floor

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.020
or in a branch of, say,
a retail operation.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.130
And you might want
to use the CI/CD

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.110
to push those things out and
actually control the builds.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.140
The other one we see a lot
is edge computing in IoT.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.690
So this is necessarily
in a situation where you

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.280
don't have great connectivity.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.960
And you might have loss of
connectivity to the Cloud,

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:34.210
but you still want to be able to
operate these clusters on-prem.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:35.679
Another one is
global availability.

00:09:35.679 --> 00:09:38.220
Your Cloud provider isn't going
to be exactly in every region

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.970
you want to be in, potentially.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.710
And so you can use GKE On-Prem,
or just a hybrid in general,

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:46.620
to provide services
in these locations.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.350
And then, closely tied to
that is the data locality.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.110
So there's a lot of restrictions
on where you can house data.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.260
And you might want to have a
policy that you can enforce.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.720
And you can place it
in a specific location.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:01.350
And finally, once
you're on-prem,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.450
you can take advantage
of a lot of the things

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.730
that your Cloud provider
offers by reaching out

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.530
from your on-prem to the Cloud.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.516
And that then
necessarily means you

00:10:10.516 --> 00:10:13.110
need to invoke Cloud
services from on-prem.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.820
So as you can see,
it's pretty clear

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.900
that you really need to be--

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.050
a lot of companies need
to be thinking about being

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.970
both on-prem and in the Cloud.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.910
They need to be thinking
about many clusters.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:25.730
So with that, I'll
say, you know, OK.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.795
Multi-cluster and hybrid
Cloud is a reality.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.900
So what?

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:34.740
It should be easy, right?

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.150
It's not.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:38.080
There's a lot of
challenges with this.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.800
So I have three things
that I'll discuss here.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.320
Workload, services,
and environment.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.450
And the first one
is the workloads.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:47.400
And there's a lot
of challenges here.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.027
So just deployment and
placement is a big challenge.

00:10:51.027 --> 00:10:52.860
You have technical and
business requirements

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.530
dictating where you're going
to deploy your services.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.060
And your data
locale requirements

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.610
are going to dictate
that, as well.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.295
You probably want to have CI/CD
integration in any solution

00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:04.920
you come up with, so
that you have best

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.990
practices around deployment.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:10.860
And then, multi-cluster
introspection is another issue.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.590
How do you ingest all
your logs into one place?

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.494
How do you monitor
every service you've

00:11:15.494 --> 00:11:17.160
deployed-- every
workload you deployed--

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.960
across many environments?

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.940
Services, as well, is
another big challenge.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:27.481
So I discussed this earlier when
I showed the multi-cluster use

00:11:27.481 --> 00:11:27.980
cases.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:31.022
But performance-- low
latency and georedundancy--

00:11:31.022 --> 00:11:32.480
that is something
that people often

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:34.730
think about when they're
placing services.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:38.000
And then this is, again, where
Istio and CSP, in general--

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.290
the Cloud Services
Platform-- come in.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.310
We've got identity and security
so you can assign identities

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.540
to your workloads.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:51.650
And you can set up access
controls across environments.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:54.250
And finally, there's
the environment.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:58.354
Compute is made a lot
easier with Kubernetes.

00:11:58.354 --> 00:11:59.770
It provides them
[INAUDIBLE] layer

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:03.910
above your cloud provider--
above your on-prem environment.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.430
But just having
that isn't enough.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:08.260
Kubernetes has many
different versions.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:10.210
If you're trying to
be on the cutting

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.810
edge of using the latest and
greatest Kubernetes features,

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:18.174
you might run into challenges
with the API compatibility.

00:12:18.174 --> 00:12:20.590
A lot of people run into issues
with OS images and cluster

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:21.510
add-ons.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:24.070
And so it's not enough just
to say I've got Kubernetes,

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.150
and I'm done.

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:28.490
And again, when we think
about identity and security,

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:30.970
there's also user identity and
security and access controls

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:33.590
that come into play here.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.360
How do I audit, say, a
user's access to a cluster?

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.660
How do we provide them
the necessary access

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:42.610
to that cluster and
to that namespace

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.193
or whatever it happens
to be that they

00:12:44.193 --> 00:12:45.262
need to access in there?

00:12:45.262 --> 00:12:46.720
And these are all
things that we're

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:50.170
going to be discussing
in the next section,

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.210
actually, with GKE On-Prem.

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.400
So with that, I'll kick
it back to you, Wes.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:55.700
WESTON HUTCHINS: I'm going
to check in with Nakheel.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:56.870
How are we doing?

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:59.510
NAKHEEL: I can register a
cluster, but not [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.497
I can register a cluster,
but not [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:02.497 --> 00:13:03.330
WESTON HUTCHINS: OK.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:05.410
We'll check back in
with you in a second.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.720
So let's talk about GKE On-Prem.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.350
I'm going to go to this side
so you guys can see over there.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.630
If you want to get
started with Kubernetes,

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.338
one of the biggest
challenges is actually

00:13:18.338 --> 00:13:20.530
getting the installation
and configuration correct.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.990
If you go to
Kubernetes.io, it usually

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:27.160
involves wading through over 40
distributions, 60 vendors that

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:28.900
are offering
Kubernetes solutions.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:33.610
And just choosing the right one
can be a task in and of itself.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:35.505
Most of the time, people
can get clusters up.

00:13:35.505 --> 00:13:36.880
I think the real
challenge that I

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:38.410
hear from a lot of
cluster operators

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.270
is how you actually
manage this long term.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:42.340
This is really where a
lot of the pain lies.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:44.860
Things like, how do you
encrypt and backup at CD?

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.950
How do you do security
patches across your system?

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.380
High availability is something
that we've been working on

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:51.490
for a very long time.

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:53.549
It's a very complicated
piece of setup

00:13:53.549 --> 00:13:55.090
where you have to
have load balancers

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.000
and redundancy
separating [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.874
CD across multiple
failure demands.

00:13:59.874 --> 00:14:02.290
And then finally, things like
managing users and policies.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.130
That's also a very
big one when you

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.130
start to not just think
about the technical bits

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:07.588
that make up a
cluster, but how you

00:14:07.588 --> 00:14:10.150
assign quota and
rate limit those out

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.760
across your organization.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.090
Almost every customer that was
using GKE that had some system

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:17.934
on-prem came to us,
and they said, well,

00:14:17.934 --> 00:14:18.850
what do you recommend?

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:22.300
And prior to this
week, we said, well,

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.910
here's some great options
out there not by Google.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.400
And they said, but what
we really want is GKE,

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:28.530
but we want it on-prem.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.100
We want it in our
own environment.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:31.390
And so that's when
the team started

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:32.848
going back and
thinking, all right.

00:14:32.848 --> 00:14:34.950
Well, maybe we can do this.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:37.750
Let's start thinking
about what it is.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.360
This is the product
for GKE On-Prem.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.460
Our real goal is to
try and figure out

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:45.130
how we can bring
turnkey, production

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:47.530
grade, easy installation--

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:49.990
the best parts of
the GKE experience--

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:52.199
to your environment.

00:14:52.199 --> 00:14:53.740
A lot of the stuff
we've been hearing

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:56.073
is like, if you can just solve
installation and upgrade,

00:14:56.073 --> 00:14:56.782
I would be happy.

00:14:56.782 --> 00:14:58.656
We want to make sure
that all of these things

00:14:58.656 --> 00:15:00.100
are tested and
validated by Google

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.980
and that we're constantly
applying security patches.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.980
But we don't want to just
stop at the Kubernetes layer.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:08.650
A lot of the stuff we're
working on our cloud platform

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:11.260
is expanding the
capabilities of GKE

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.580
to offer other higher
level services.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:16.980
So things like Google Cloud
Build Container Registry,

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.640
we have vulnerability
scanning, we

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.650
have a number of other features
around, like audit logging.

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:25.810
We want to make these features
that customers can use for GKE

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:30.720
and Google Cloud Platform also
available for our GKE On-Prem

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.170
product.

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:35.200
And just as I intro-ed,
Cloud Services Platform

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.302
is really sitting on top of this
base layer that is GKE On-Prem.

00:15:39.302 --> 00:15:41.260
We want to make sure that
we can offer not just

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.990
Kubernetes, but things
like service abstractions,

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:47.290
things like developer tools, and
paths like components that you

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:48.880
can use to give to
your developers.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.379
And that's where Knative, Istio,
and some of the marketplace

00:15:51.379 --> 00:15:53.020
solutions come in.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.234
And that alluded to this.

00:15:54.234 --> 00:15:55.650
We hear a lot of
people say, well,

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.060
the most important thing is
really getting consistent.

00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:59.980
It's first off
just the tool stack

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.390
that they use to provision
and upgrade Kubernetes.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:06.220
But it's also ensuring that
you have conformant Kubernetes,

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.600
that you have the same add-ons
deployed in every cluster,

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:11.380
you have the same
configuration in every cluster.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:12.880
People really want
high confidence

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.970
that you can take a workload
and make it portable.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.890
And the one real
way to do that is

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:20.530
ensure that your environments
are consistent no matter where

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.150
you're deploying.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.290
So for installation
and configuration,

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:26.770
the early focus
for us has really

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.710
been getting on a
vSphere environment

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:32.680
for our alpha, which is going
to be available in the fall.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:34.810
The primitives are really
trying to come up with,

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.351
what is that GKE experience
that we can offer?

00:16:38.351 --> 00:16:39.850
For the early
versions that we have,

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:42.610
we've been focusing on
an ICLI installation,

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:46.390
supporting things like online
and offline installation,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.850
private container registry
support for people

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:50.550
that are behind firewalls.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:54.610
And I think one of the most
important pieces of this

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.740
is the customers that we've been
talking to want to move fast.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.280
They don't want to have
year-long upgrade cycles.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:04.660
They see the pace with which we
move with Kubernetes and GKE.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.160
And we want to help them
move at that pace as well.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.550
So moving on the latest
three versions of Kubernetes

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:11.440
and keeping that
rolling window updated

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:14.357
is such a big core component
of what we're trying to offer.

00:17:14.357 --> 00:17:16.690
And then we want to take some
of the great features that

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:18.148
we've been working
on for the GKE--

00:17:18.148 --> 00:17:20.710
things around high availability
and automatic repair

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:21.880
of your nodes--

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:25.109
to your on-prem environment.

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:28.200
So Nakheel-- I'm going to
check back in on the demo.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.250
How are we doing?

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:30.840
NAKHEEL: I can
register a cluster.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:31.500
WESTON HUTCHINS:
Should we do that?

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:32.280
NAKHEEL: Yep.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:32.740
WESTON HUTCHINS: All right.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:33.750
I'm going to kick it
over to Nakheel, who's

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.041
going to show you a quick demo.

00:17:37.285 --> 00:17:38.076
NAKHEEL: All right.

00:17:38.076 --> 00:17:41.809
So we have GKE On-Prem, which
you can have your own cluster.

00:17:41.809 --> 00:17:44.100
But then the next step is,
and really the powerful step

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.470
is, when you connect it
to Google Cloud Console.

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.420
So here, I'm showing
you a Cloud Console.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.280
And I'm in the clusters view.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.710
And again, before now, you
can list your GKE clusters.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:55.650
And you see all
the cluster there.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:57.870
You can go to the workloads
UI and see our workloads

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.370
on GKE cluster.

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:02.310
But now what you'll see is we
have this additional register

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:03.840
external cluster.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.370
So what you can do
using this is you

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:09.400
can register your on-prem GKE
cluster with Cloud Console.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:12.400
So let's try that out.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.830
I'm going to register
external cluster.

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:17.160
It gives me two options.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:19.370
I can use public
internet connection

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:20.420
to register my cluster.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:23.340
Or I can use GKE connect.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.410
Let's try GKE connect.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.150
What it's going
to do is it'll ask

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:30.780
me to install a connect
agent into my cluster which

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:34.170
will establish a tunnel from
the cluster to Cloud Console

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:37.740
so that Cloud Console can then
send requests to the cluster.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.390
So let's give my cluster a name.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:41.890
I wanted to give
it On-prem Cluster.

00:18:44.482 --> 00:18:46.910
And now, I download the
manifest which I have

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:48.160
to install into my cluster.

00:18:53.084 --> 00:18:55.000
So let's look at what
the manifest looks like.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.090
The manifest has
a service account

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:01.410
that the agent will use.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.530
It has cluster role binding,
and it has a deployment.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.030
This deployment
is the agent that

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.970
would run-- that would establish
the tunnel from the cluster

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.630
to Cloud Console.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:12.425
So let's go ahead and apply it.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:21.070
You can see it created a
service account, cluster role

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.044
binding, and a deployment.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.480
And you can see this
is waiting right now.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:30.669
And hopefully-- fingers
crossed-- if the demo works,

00:19:30.669 --> 00:19:32.210
it should show us
a green check mark.

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:32.710
Awesome.

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:33.820
It works.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.650
So now, this shows
that the agent

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:38.500
was able to set up a
connection from the cluster

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.510
to Cloud Console.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:44.200
So let's try the next step.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.260
Now Cloud Console needs
some more information,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.580
which is basically the kube
config information for it

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:52.580
to be able to send Kubernetes
API requests to the cluster.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.400
So let's copy our kube config.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:15.800
As you can see in this case,
I'm using a Minikube cluster.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.140
It could be a GKE
On-Prem cluster.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:22.580
It could be a cluster
you brought up yourself.

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:24.590
It could be any Kubernetes
cluster, as long

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:26.400
as it uses the Kubernetes API.

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:32.510
So you go ahead
and authenticate.

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:33.880
And skip authentication for now.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:35.620
Just let's see
registration works.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:39.470
Awesome.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:42.130
Now we see in the same
cluster's list view,

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:44.800
you have your GKE cluster,
which is the East Coast.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.640
And you also have
your on-prem cluster.

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:51.160
If I go to workloads, I
continue to see workloads

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:52.390
from my East Coast cluster.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.230
And ideally, I should see
workloads from this new cluster

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:56.500
that I just registered.

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:59.420
But since I haven't logged
into it, I don't see it.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.119
So let's try to log into it.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:24.520
I'm sorry I have to
type all of this.

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:31.446
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:21:31.446 --> 00:21:33.070
to use the token of
the service account

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:34.790
that I just created
for my agent.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:47.860
So I can use basic
auth or token.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:49.464
In this case, I'm
going to use token.

00:21:54.201 --> 00:21:54.700
And, voila.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:55.354
It works.

00:21:55.354 --> 00:21:57.270
Now you can see workloads
from your East Coast

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.110
cluster and your
on-prem cluster,

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:00.850
all in the workloads view.

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:04.350
If I go back to
my clusters list,

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:06.810
now you can also see total
[INAUDIBLE],, total memory.

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:07.893
Earlier, it was all blank.

00:22:07.893 --> 00:22:10.090
Now it can access
all this information.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:11.790
If I click on my
on-prem cluster,

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.410
it shows me even
more information--

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.840
this is a [INAUDIBLE]
cluster that's running--

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:20.420
what old storage information
I have, what unloads I have.

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:22.790
In this case, I have just
one cluster, one load, which

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:24.050
is the Minikube API server.

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:25.520
I can go dig deeper.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.190
So this now empowers me to
use all the Cloud Console

00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:31.130
functionality that I was able
to use with a GKE server now.

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:33.790
I can now use it with my
any other cluster, as well,

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.524
which is great.

00:22:35.524 --> 00:22:36.440
WESTON HUTCHINS: Cool.

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:37.440
Thanks, Nakheel.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:38.790
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:44.730
We had the demo working
on the rack earlier.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:45.780
Technical SNAFU.

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:47.550
But, thank you for that.

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:50.880
It shows not just that we
can offer the GKE experience

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:51.652
on the box.

00:22:51.652 --> 00:22:53.610
But we can actually take
any Kubernetes cluster

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:56.810
and register with our
Cloud Console now.

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.340
So the question I often get
is, like, what does management

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:01.570
mean for GKE On-Prem?

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:04.390
A lot of the stuff
that we want to offer

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:08.110
is a Google managed experience,
both through tooling,

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:10.450
but also through a persistent
connection with our Cloud

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.991
Console where we can monitor
and health check and keep things

00:23:12.991 --> 00:23:13.750
up to date.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:16.660
So part of what we're
trying to go out with

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.910
is both an option where
Google manages your cluster,

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.310
and also, we have partner
supports that can help deploy

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.590
these in other environments.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:26.080
But even outside
of that, we want

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:28.690
to provide extra tooling
above and beyond even what we

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:30.070
have for some of the GKE stuff.

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.990
So we want to make sure that we
can get the right metrics out

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.420
of the cluster, that we
have automatic repair.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:36.910
And we even offer some
node management features

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:39.790
such as Node Auto-Upgrades
and Node Repair

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:44.830
that we offer as part of our
GKE on the Cloud experience.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:48.060
Now logging and monitoring is
a little different in the sense

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.430
that we know that
a lot of people

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:53.520
have to have a local logmon
experience for doing debugging.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.600
We don't want to have to egress
everything back to the cloud.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.320
But we do know some people
want the best of both worlds.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.750
So what we're offering
is a pre-built

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:04.920
in cluster dashboard
based off of Prometheus

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.200
[INAUDIBLE] CaseStack.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:11.414
And after you do that, you
can browse everything locally.

00:24:11.414 --> 00:24:13.080
You can have the
metrics in your cluster

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.460
and do some local debugging.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.130
You can then install
Stackdriver agents

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:19.080
and register with
Google Cloud Console

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:23.820
and have all of these
metrics ingested into GCP.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.941
This is what we demoed
at the keynote on day one

00:24:25.941 --> 00:24:28.440
where we were actually showing
metrics coming in and showing

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.390
up on top of the service graph.

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:33.210
Now, the real big
power of this is

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:36.570
we want people to use the same
tools to manage both their GKE

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.220
clusters in the
cloud and on-prem.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:40.650
So we want Stackdriver to
be that place where people

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:44.220
go to set alerts and configure
dashboards so that they

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:47.076
can view both clusters
in the same place.

00:24:47.076 --> 00:24:48.450
The best part is
that Stackdriver

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:50.616
supports Prometheus as a
data store under the cover.

00:24:50.616 --> 00:24:54.350
So there's no extra
instrumentation required.

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:57.230
Identity is an area
where we want--

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:58.880
we're working with
a lot of customers

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:02.030
and trying to figure out how
we can meet their needs on-prem

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:03.410
and also move them to the cloud.

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:05.832
So we know that Active
Directory is going

00:25:05.832 --> 00:25:07.040
to be a part of our solution.

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:09.770
We want to integrate natively
with people's local Active

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:11.222
Directory trees.

00:25:11.222 --> 00:25:12.680
But for every
feature that you need

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:16.890
to use in Google Cloud Platform,
we need to have a GCP identity.

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:19.130
So we're exploring options,
things like Google Cloud

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.020
Directory sync, things
like provisioning

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:23.180
a set of users for use
in our Cloud Console

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:25.759
and all of the GCP services.

00:25:25.759 --> 00:25:28.300
We'll have more details about
some of this stuff a little bit

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:28.800
later on.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.133
The other thing that we know
customers are going to want

00:25:31.133 --> 00:25:33.400
is the ability to use
their own cert authority

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:34.960
and their own on-prem cluster.

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:36.900
So we're going to
support the ability

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:41.450
to bring your own public
key infrastructure.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:44.780
Another part of CSP
that we unveiled at Next

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:46.860
is policy management.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:50.480
So this is a feature that
allows you to create and define

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.180
policies using a
declarative configuration,

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:55.910
check those into Git
as a source of truth,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:59.000
and then federate and sync those
across every cluster that you

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:01.440
have, whether they're running
in the Cloud and on-prem.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.460
So this allows you to
do things like set up

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:07.190
how you want your users to
access all of the clusters

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:09.570
and then manage them
centrally through Google Cloud

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:12.390
Console and the policy
management syncing tools.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:14.360
There's a bunch
more depth that's

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:16.700
going to be covered
at a session tomorrow

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:18.340
with Ray Colline
and John Murray.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:21.380
And that-- Thursday at
11:45 in Moscone West

00:26:21.380 --> 00:26:25.220
if you want to hear more
about this cool new feature.

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.830
And then Nakheel was
saying before, we really

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:31.520
want Cloud Console to be the
place that people come in

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:33.230
and manage all of
their workloads

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:36.500
and manage all of their clusters
from a single pane of glass UI.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:38.520
GKE On-Prem is special.

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:40.880
This is a Google-managed
Kubernetes offering.

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.560
And that's going to
register seamlessly

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:43.790
with Google Cloud Console.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:45.800
But there will also
have the ability

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.110
to take any Kubernetes
cluster, whether they're

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:50.990
running on Minikube locally, or
even in another cloud provider,

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:56.140
and register it with our single
pane of glass solution, which

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:56.890
is what we demoed.

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:58.931
So we're going to skip
that part of it right now.

00:26:58.931 --> 00:27:00.940
And Matt is going to tell
you a little bit more

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:02.680
about the GKE connect agents.

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:10.180
MATT DE LEO: Here we go.

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:10.680
All right.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:17.200
So we quickly showed you a demo
of us connecting from Minikube

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:18.690
back to GCP.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.240
And I want to just do a quick
dive into what we actually did.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:24.000
So Nakheel mentioned we
installed this connection agent

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.470
onto the cluster.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:28.230
And what this is is
just a deployment

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:33.630
that dials out from
the cluster to Google

00:27:33.630 --> 00:27:35.950
and creates a long
live connection.

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:39.390
Once we have that
connection in place,

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:42.720
we have the ability
then to go back

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.550
through the connection
the other way

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:47.560
and provide you this single
pane of glass experience.

00:27:47.560 --> 00:27:51.960
So when an end user comes in and
they talk to the Cloud Console,

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:55.560
their request to view
workload on the cluster

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:57.660
will route through
that connection, back

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:00.480
through the agent, and
ultimately to the API server.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.600
The reason we did this
is probably obvious.

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:06.850
But if it's not, then I'll
do a quick explanation here.

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:10.080
So one is, we don't
really want people

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:12.480
to have to expose their
clusters to the public internet

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:14.130
if they're running on-prem.

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:16.950
And by having this
agent reach out

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.980
from there on-prem environment
or your other cloud provider

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:21.870
environment to
Google Cloud, we can

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:25.470
create an encrypted
connection back to GCP.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:28.570
And it can traverse [INAUDIBLE]
and firewall rules--

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:31.110
sorry, egress firewalls.

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:32.880
We have proxy support.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:34.800
And we can create that
long live connection

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:40.620
and provide a secure way to
access your cluster remotely.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:42.840
The nice part about
this also is because we

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:46.260
are passing the end users--

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.540
so you saw the page that
Nakheel showed when we

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:50.460
went to log into the cluster.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.486
We want every user to log in
with their own credentials.

00:28:53.486 --> 00:28:55.110
And the reason we
want to do that is we

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:57.060
want your audit
logs in your cluster

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:59.520
to show a good audit
trail of what's

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:00.750
happening in that cluster.

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:02.670
So when the first person--

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.580
the administrator-- registers
a cluster, all that happens

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:07.020
is it shows up for everybody.

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:09.660
And everybody has to come
in and log in separately.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.940
And they use their end user
credentials, like I said,

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:14.250
we're going to have really
good audit logs that end up

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:14.874
in the cluster.

00:29:14.874 --> 00:29:16.800
So it makes auditing
really, really good.

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:22.890
So Wes mentioned also that
this doesn't extend to just GKE

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:25.050
On-Prem or other GKE clusters.

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:28.860
It really extends to bring
your own Kubernetes clusters.

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:30.937
And once we have
that agent installed,

00:29:30.937 --> 00:29:32.020
we can do a lot of things.

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:33.728
So we mentioned the
single pane of glass.

00:29:33.728 --> 00:29:36.430
But we're looking at integrating
with a lot of other services,

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:39.420
including the marketplace
and Stackdriver and a bunch

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:42.870
of other things to provide
the GKE experience really

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:47.700
for a lot of clusters
that aren't even GKE.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.700
And with that, we'll wrap it up.

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:52.620
There's a couple
things here I'll note.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:55.560
So one is that GKE On-Prem--

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:58.350
Kubernetes On-Prem-- is going
to be available in alpha

00:29:58.350 --> 00:29:59.850
this fall.

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:02.589
And also that we
have a sign-up form.

00:30:02.589 --> 00:30:03.880
I think we're missing the link.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.840
So we'll put that up
right before we wrap.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:09.630
But that's pretty much it.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.490
So we'll take
questions offstage.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.260
And thank you very
much for coming.

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:15.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

